@startuml Device Onboarding Flow
!theme plain
skinparam backgroundColor #0f1f15
skinparam defaultFontColor #e0f0e8
skinparam defaultFontSize 13

skinparam participant {
  BackgroundColor #1e2d24
  BorderColor #3a5a45
  FontColor #e0f0e8
}
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #1a7f4b
  LifeLineBorderColor #3a5a45
  LifeLineBackgroundColor #162a1e
  DividerBackgroundColor #1e2d24
  DividerBorderColor #3a5a45
  DividerFontColor #a0c8b0
  GroupBorderColor #3a5a45
  GroupBackgroundColor #162a1e
  GroupFontColor #a0c8b0
  NoteBackgroundColor #1e2d24
  NoteBorderColor #3a5a45
  NoteFontColor #7aab90
}
skinparam shadowing false

title Device Onboarding — New Site (e.g. "Home 1")

actor       "Owner"       as User   #1a7f4b
participant "PWA\n(React)" as PWA   #1e2d24
participant "Firebase\nRTDB"        as FB    #2a3d5a
participant "ESP32\nFirmware"       as ESP   #2e1e0f

== Step 1 — Create Site in PWA ==

User  -> PWA : Tap "+ Site", enter name "Home 1"
PWA   -> FB  : push( irrigation/sites ) → new site key
FB   --> PWA : site key returned (e.g. -OAbc123)
PWA   -> PWA : setSiteId( -OAbc123 )\nstored in localStorage

note over PWA
  Dashboard, Control, Schedule, Map
  all switch to the new empty site.
  Zones list = [] until device is added.
end note

== Step 2 — Flash ESP32 Firmware ==

User  -> ESP  : Open Arduino IDE,\nflash irrigation_main.ino\nwith config.h pointing to this Firebase project
note over ESP
  config.h must contain:
  • FIREBASE_API_KEY
  • FIREBASE_EMAIL / PASSWORD
  • FIREBASE_DB_URL
  • ZONE_1_ID = "zone-a"
  • ZONE_2_ID = "zone-b"  (or any IDs)
end note

== Step 3 — WiFi Provisioning (Captive Portal) ==

ESP   -> ESP  : Boot — no WiFi creds in Preferences flash
ESP   -> ESP  : Start AP mode\nSSID: ESP32_Config\nPW:   123456789

User  -> ESP  : Phone connects to "ESP32_Config" AP
User  -> ESP  : Browser opens 192.168.4.1\nEnters home WiFi SSID + password
ESP   -> ESP  : Save SSID + password to Preferences (flash)
ESP   -> ESP  : Reboot

note over ESP
  On all future boots, creds are read from
  flash — provisioning page never shown again
  unless flash is cleared.
end note

== Step 4 — Device Connects to Firebase ==

ESP   -> ESP  : Read creds from Preferences,\nWiFi.begin( SSID, password )
ESP   -> FB   : Firebase.begin( config )\nAuthenticate with email/password
FB   --> ESP  : Auth token granted
ESP   -> FB   : Write devices/esp32-01\n{ firmware, ipAddress, wifiRssi, lastSeen }

note over FB
  Device heartbeat path:
  irrigation/sites/{siteId}/devices/esp32-01/lastSeen
end note

== Step 5 — Zone Bootstrap (first publish) ==

ESP   -> FB   : set  zones/{zone-a}/meta\n{ name:"Zone A", enabled:true }
ESP   -> FB   : set  zones/{zone-b}/meta\n{ name:"Zone B", enabled:true }
ESP   -> FB   : set  zones/{zone-a}/sensor\n{ moisturePercent, rawValue, timestamp }
ESP   -> FB   : push zones/{zone-a}/history  ← first reading
ESP   -> FB   : set  zones/{zone-b}/sensor   ← same for zone B
ESP   -> FB   : push zones/{zone-b}/history

FB   --> PWA  : onValue() fires — zones list updates
PWA  --> User : Dashboard shows Zone A & Zone B cards\nDevice status bar → ONLINE

== Step 6 — Pin Zones on Map ==

User  -> PWA  : Open Map tab → tap "+ Pin Zone A"
User  -> PWA  : Tap garden position on SVG map
PWA   -> FB   : set  zones/{zone-a}/meta\n{ ...existing, px:45, py:30 }
User  -> PWA  : Tap "+ Pin Zone B", tap another position
PWA   -> FB   : set  zones/{zone-b}/meta\n{ ...existing, px:55, py:70 }

note over PWA
  Pins are colour-coded by live moisture.
  Blue pulse ring = valve currently OPEN.
end note

== Step 7 — Configure Alerts & Schedule ==

User  -> PWA  : Alerts tab → enter Telegram Bot Token + Chat ID → Save
PWA   -> FB   : set  settings/alerts\n{ telegram, zones: { threshold, enabled }, offlineMinutes }

User  -> PWA  : Schedule tab → add 06:00 / 18:00 entries per zone
PWA   -> FB   : push zones/{zone-a}/schedule/{ hour, minute, durationMinutes, days, enabled }
PWA   -> FB   : push zones/{zone-b}/schedule/{ ... }
ESP   -> FB   : onValue() on schedule — loads entries on next tick

note over User, ESP
  Onboarding complete.
  ESP32 now autonomously reads sensors every 30 s,
  follows schedule, and reacts to manual valve commands.
  Valve safety cutoff: 10 min regardless of any command.
end note

@enduml
